  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1110 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air analysis late Friday \nevening reveal southwesterly flow is in place across the central \nRockies/plains between a strong cut-off low centered over southern \nIdaho and a ~594-dm ridge downstream over the southern plains. At \nthe surface, an elongated area of low pressure extends from east-\ncentral CO northeastward through northwest KS and into south-central \nNE, separating warm temperatures to the south and cool temperatures \nto the north. As the upper level low inches eastward daytime \nSaturday, the surface low will sag southward, allowing a cold front \nto move slowly through southwest KS. Southwesterly downslope flow \nahead of this boundary will yield one final day of summer-like \ntemperatures with highs in the mid/upper 90s for roughly the \nsoutheastern half of our CWA while the northwestern half begins to \ncool off within the post-frontal northerlies with highs in the mid \n80s to low 90s. This cold front may also serve as the focus for \nafternoon thunderstorm development, both along/ahead of it as well \nas within the post-frontal upslope flow regime in southeast CO. Any \nconvection that develops along the boundary or meanders into our \narea from southeast CO will carry a marginal severe threat given the \npresence of around 1000 J/Kg of MLCAPE and ~30 kts of deep layer \nshear. The primary threats associated with this activity will be \nlarge hail and damaging wind gusts. Elevated convection may persist \ninto the overnight period sustained by 850-700-mb warm advection, \nbut these will pose a decreasing severe threat with time. Otherwise, \ncold advection will promote Sunday morning lows dropping into the \nupper 50s/low 60s northwest to upper 60s southeast.\n\nDaytime Sunday, the upper level trough will eject onto the northern \nplains as surface high pressure settles into the central plains \nbehind the cold front, and at long last, most of southwest KS will \nenjoy cool temperatures as afternoon highs peak in the 70s. Some \nareas along/near the KS/OK border may still reach into the low to \nmid 80s owing to a more shallow cool airmass, but this will still be \na welcome change from the upper 90s/near 100 heat these areas have \nbeen mired in for months.\n\nMonday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles agree \nthe cool temperatures are here to stay as NBM highs remain in the \n70s to mid-80s range through at least the end of the business week. \nEnsembles also suggest that precipitation chances will exist most, \nif not all days, favoring our western zones. Highest probability of \nwidespread precipitation currently appears to surround Thursday \nevening and overnight as the NBM paints chance pops (25-54%) across \nnearly our entire area.",
